## 2. Dataset Collection & Automated Web Scraping Strategy

Creating custom dataset annotations manually is tedious. We can automate dataset generation using web scraping combined with **Zero-Shot Auto-Labeling (Grounding DINO / Florence-2)**.

```
+----------------------+     Image Downloads     +-----------------------+
|  Internet Scraping   |  -------------------->  | Zero-Shot Auto-Label  |
|  (DuckDuckGo/Bing)   |                         | (Grounding DINO)      |
+----------------------+                         +-----------------------+
                                                             |
                                                             v
+----------------------+     YOLO Dataset format |  Auto-Generated       |
| Custom YOLO Model    |  <--------------------  |  Bounding Boxes &     |
| Training (Local/Cloud)|                        |  Labels (dataset.yaml)|
+----------------------+                         +-----------------------+
```

### Automated Workflow Step-by-Step

#### Step A: Multi-Keyword Image Scraping Script (`scripts/fetch_ingredient_images.py`)
Using `icrawler` or `bing-image-downloader` to pull ~100–200 diverse images per ingredient:
- Query variations: `raw garlic clove`, `peeled garlic`, `chopped garlic`, `chicken breast raw`, `diced chicken breast`, `bell pepper red`, `soy sauce bottle`.
- Filter out tiny (< 300x300), corrupted, or duplicate images automatically (using perceptual hash `imagehash`).

#### Step B: Zero-Shot Automated Bounding Box Labeling (`scripts/auto_label_dataset.py`)
Instead of drawing boxes manually:
- Use **Grounding DINO** or **Florence-2** open-vocabulary detector.
- Input prompt: `"garlic", "chicken breast", "bell pepper", "soy sauce"`.
- Script automatically outputs YOLO format `.txt` label files (`class_id x_center y_center width height`) for every scraped image!
- Optional: Quick inspection & validation via **Roboflow** or **CVAT** to clean up any noisy labels.

#### Step C: Dataset Structure & Augmentation
Organize into standard Ultralytics YOLO structure:
```
data/dataset/
├── dataset.yaml
├── images/
│   ├── train/
│   └── val/
└── labels/
    ├── train/
    └── val/
```
**`dataset.yaml` example:**
```yaml
path: data/dataset
train: images/train
val: images/val
names:
  0: garlic
  1: chicken_breast
  2: bell_pepper
  3: soy_sauce
```

